e": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 285, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 87, "itemDescription": "The off-price department store chain brought in nearly $3.5 billion in profits in 2022, a 7% increase over 2021. TJX dropped 12 spots on the Fortune 500 list after a 22-spot jump last year.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>Marmaxx, TJX\u2019s apparel unit, was its biggest revenue driver, bringing in $30 billion of the $49.9 billion the company generated overall. Its HomeGoods division, however, saw slower sales after two years of expansion during the pandemic.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>TJX continues to lag in the e-commerce space, which held the company back at COVID\u2019s height.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Still, TJX has outlasted many of its competitors; Bed Bath &amp; Beyond\u2019s bankruptcy may lead to more foot traffic at TJX stores.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 280, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 75, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">TJX is the parent company of off-price retailers like T.J.Maxx, Marshalls, HomeGoods, Sierra, and Homesense, operating almost 4,700 stores in nine countries. The pandemic initially had a severe impact on revenue for TJX, which gets only a small percentage of its sales from e-commerce. But as lockdowns ended, the company rebounded, and in 2021, it grew revenue by 51% year over year, enabling it to jump 22 spots on this year\u2019s list. In March 2022, TJX announced it would sell its 25% stake in Russian apparel retailer Familia in response to Russia\u2019s invasion of Ukraine. In April 2022, the company announced that it is aiming to achieve net-zero greenhouse gas emissions in its operations by 2040.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 383, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;TJX took a particularly brutal beating from the pandemic last year, hit by the double whammy of having almost no e-commerce to fall back on when its stores were closed for long periods, and the precipitous drop in spending on fashion. Sales fell 23% to $32.1 billion\u2014but the damage could have been worse. For one thing TJX\u2019s HomeGoods business slipped only 4%. And when its other banners, T.J. Maxx and Marshalls, reopened, business rebounded quickly. There are signs of pent-up demand for fashion apparel that should give TJX a boost this year (barring any new outbreaks that close stores en masse again), and spending on the home continues apace.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14723,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;4&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:16777215},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">TJX took a particularly brutal beating from the pandemic last year, hit by the double whammy of having almost no e-commerce to fall back on when its stores were closed for long periods, and the precipitous drop in spending on fashion. Sales fell 23% to $32.1 billion\u2014but the damage could have been worse. For one thing TJX\u2019s HomeGoods business slipped only 4%. And when its other banners, T.J. Maxx and Marshalls, reopened, business rebounded quickly. There are signs of pent-up demand for fashion apparel that should give TJX a boost this year (barring any new outbreaks that close stores en masse again), and spending on the home continues apace.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 97, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;TJX took a particularly brutal beating from the pandemic last year, hit by the double whammy of having almost no e-commerce to fall back on when its stores were closed for long periods, and the precipitous drop in spending on fashion. Sales fell 23% to $32.1 billion\u2014but the damage could have been worse. For one thing TJX\u2019s HomeGoods business slipped only 4%. And when its other banners, T.J. Maxx and Marshalls, reopened, business rebounded quickly. There are signs of pent-up demand for fashion apparel that should give TJX a boost this year (barring any new outbreaks that close stores en masse again), and spending on the home continues apace.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14723,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;4&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:16777215},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">TJX took a particularly brutal beating from the pandemic last year, hit by the double whammy of having almost no e-commerce to fall back on when its stores were closed for long periods, and the precipitous drop in spending on fashion. Sales fell 23% to $32.1 billion\u2014but the damage could have been worse. For one thing TJX\u2019s HomeGoods business slipped only 4%. And when its other banners, T.J. Maxx and Marshalls, reopened, business rebounded quickly. There are signs of pent-up demand for fashion apparel that should give TJX a boost this year (barring any new outbreaks that close stores en masse again), and spending on the home continues apace.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 292, "itemDescription": "The off-price discount fashion and home goods retailer continued its long march toward retail dominance, with sales up 7% in 2019, to $41.7 billion, making it bigger than Macy\u2019s and J.C. Penney combined. In the early days of the pandemic, TJX revenues fell off nearly entirely, with all stores closed and only a tiny e-commerce business in operation. But coming out of the lockdowns, TJX's strong financials should make its stores even more desirable to fashion brands as department stores continue to wither, analysts say. What\u2019s more, TJX\u2019s home furnishings chain HomeGoods has been booming alongside the more apparel-oriented T.J. Maxx and Marshalls."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 80, "itemDescription": "The off-price discount fashion and home goods retailer continued its long march toward retail dominance, with sales up 7% in 2019, to $41.7 billion, making it bigger than Macy\u2019s and J.C. Penney combined. In the early days of the pandemic, TJX revenues fell off nearly entirely, with all stores closed and only a tiny e-commerce business in operation. But coming out of the lockdowns, TJX's strong financials will make its stores even more desirable to fashion brands as department stores continue to wither. What\u2019s more, TJX\u2019s home furnishings chain Home Goods has been booming alongside the more apparel-oriented T.J. Maxx and Marshalls."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 317, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 85, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2